1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,990
The screen turns toward you.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,990
Three suggested tips.

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,990
Maybe a small button marked "No tip."

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,990
The worker waits nearby.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,990
Someone is behind you.

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,990
Before you can put away your card,

7
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:12,990
a coffee purchase has become a public

8
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,990
question about whether you are fair.

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,990
A tip rewards good service.

10
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:18,990
It is voluntary.

11
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,990
You decide.

12
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,990
But if the payment is truly optional,

13
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,990
why can choosing zero feel like refusing

14
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,990
to pay part of the bill?

15
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,990
Because tipping is not only a thank-you.

16
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,990
In the United States,

17
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,990
it can also be a decentralized wage decision,

18
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,990
made by one customer at a time,

19
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,990
under social pressure and product design.

20
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,990
Start with one ordinary transaction.

21
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,990
Your restaurant bill is twenty dollars.

22
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,990
Using the 15.4 percent average reported across

23
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,990
Square restaurant transactions in 2024,

24
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,990
the tip is three dollars and eight cents.

25
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,990
Your total becomes twenty-three dollars and eight cents.

26
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,990
That extra three dollars and eight cents looks simple.

27
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,990
Money leaves your account and,

28
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,990
subject to the rules of the workplace,

29
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,990
helps compensate the people serving you.

30
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,990
But the menu gave you one price,

31
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,990
while the social system asked you to calculate another.

32
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,990
The final labor cost was partly left

33
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,990
open until the last moment.

34
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,990
This arrangement gives each participant a reasonable

35
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:19,990
reason to keep it going.

36
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,990
The worker wants reliable income.

37
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,990
Tips can be a meaningful part of it.

38
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,990
In Square's 2024 data,

39
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,990
the average restaurant worker earned nearly

40
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,990
23 percent of income from tips.

41
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,990
That figure describes workers in Square's data,

42
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,990
not every restaurant worker in America.

43
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,990
Still, it shows why the choice on your

44
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,990
screen may matter to the person across the counter.

45
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,990
The employer has to attract workers,

46
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,990
manage payroll, and keep prices competitive.

47
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:50,990
In some jurisdictions,

48
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,990
tips can count toward part

49
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,990
of the employer's minimum-wage obligation.

50
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,990
A tip prompt can also make additional compensation visible

51
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,990
without folding it into the posted menu price.

52
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,990
The payment platform wants checkout to work for the merchant.

53
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,990
It provides the interface and the possible tip settings.

54
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,990
The business controls how that checkout is configured.

55
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,990
You control the final choice,

56
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,990
but only after someone else has designed the question.

57
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:17,990
And you want to be fair.

58
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,990
You do not want to underpay someone who helped you.

59
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:22,990
You also do not want every purchase

60
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,990
to become a negotiation over wages.

61
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,990
So you look for a rule.

62
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,990
That is where the behavioral engine appears.

63
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,990
Tipping runs on norm

64
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,990
compliance under ambiguity.

65
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,990
When the correct action is unclear,

66
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,990
people look for a shared expectation.

67
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,990
The screen supplies possible answers.

68
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,990
The worker's presence makes the decision feel interpersonal.

69
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,990
The line behind you adds urgency.

70
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,990
None of this removes your ability to choose.

71
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,990
It changes how the choice feels.

72
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,990
Americans themselves describe that uncertainty.

73
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,990
In a nationally representative

74
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,990
2023 survey,

75
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,990
72 percent of adults said

76
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:00,990
tipping was expected in more

77
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,990
places than it had been five years earlier.

78
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,990
Yet only 34 percent said it was

79
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,990
very or extremely easy to know

80
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:09,990
whether to tip.

81
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,990
Nearly half said whether tipping is a choice

82
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,990
or an obligation depends on the situation.

83
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,990
That is an unusually powerful position

84
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:18,990
for a payment to occupy.

85
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,990
It is legally voluntary,

86
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,990
socially conditional,

87
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,990
and economically meaningful to the person receiving it.

88
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,990
The suggested amounts matter too,

89
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,990
although not in one universal way.

90
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:32,990
A field experiment involving more than ninety-four

91
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,990
thousand orders for an app-based laundry service

92
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,990
found that randomly assigned tip recommendations changed

93
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:40,990
tip amounts.

94
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:41,990
In that setting,

95
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,990
the recommendations did not change satisfaction,

96
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,990
return frequency,

97
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:47,990
or bill size.

98
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:48,990
But another study,

99
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,990
using a simulated limited-service coffee shop,

100
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:53,990
found that screen-based payment methods

101
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,990
produced more negative feelings

102
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:56,990
than tip jars without

103
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,990
increasing tip sizes.

104
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,990
Because that study used hypothetical choices,

105
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:02,990
it cannot tell us exactly what

106
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,990
happens in every real cafe.

107
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,990
What it does show is the tension:

108
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,990
a prompt can affect the experience

109
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,990
even when it does not produce more money.

110
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,990
So the hidden cost is not just the dollars.

111
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,990
It is the conversion of an ordinary checkout into a small

112
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:18,990
moral decision.

113
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,990
Now comes the distinction that changes the category.

114
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,990
A tip and a mandatory service charge

115
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,990
may appear on the same receipt,

116
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:27,990
but under federal rules

117
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,990
they are not the same kind of payment.

118
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:31,990
For federal tax purposes,

119
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,990
a tip is voluntary.

120
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,990
The customer decides whether to pay it and determines the amount.

121
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,990
A compulsory service charge is different.

122
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,990
It is a required part of the bill,

123
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,990
treated as income to the employer and

124
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,990
generally as wages when distributed to employees.

125
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,990
Even the design of a digital prompt can cross that line.

126
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,990
If a screen requires you to select an amount greater than zero,

127
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:56,990
the IRS

128
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,990
gives that as an example of a service charge,

129
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:00,990
not a tip.

130
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,990
The zero option matters because it

131
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,990
separates a suggestion from a price.

132
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,990
This means the awkwardness at checkout

133
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,990
is not an accidental side effect.

134
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:10,990
It sits exactly on the

135
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,990
boundary that defines the transaction.

136
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,990
The payment must remain voluntary to be a tip,

137
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,990
yet the compensation system works best when

138
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,990
enough customers feel expected to pay it.

139
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,990
The clearest case study is the federal tip credit.

140
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,990
Under the federal Fair Labor Standards Act,

141
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,990
an eligible employer using the tip credit

142
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,990
may pay a tipped worker a direct cash wage of

143
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,990
at least two dollars and thirteen cents per hour.

144
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,990
The employer may count up to five

145
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,990
dollars and twelve cents per hour

146
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,990
in tips toward the federal minimum wage

147
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,990
of seven dollars and twenty-five cents.

148
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:45,990
That does not mean the worker can

149
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,990
legally be left below the federal minimum.

150
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:49,990
If wages and tips do not reach

151
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,990
the required amount over the workweek,

152
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,990
the employer must make up the difference.

153
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,990
State rules may require a higher direct wage

154
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,990
or prohibit the tip credit entirely.

155
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:01,990
The point is not that your individual

156
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,990
tip erases the employer's responsibility.

157
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:06,990
It does not.

158
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,990
The point is that, where the tip credit applies,

159
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,990
customer tips are formally connected

160
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,990
to the employer's wage obligation.

161
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,990
A payment presented as personal gratitude also

162
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,990
participates in a regulated compensation system.

163
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,990
This helps explain why reasonable people on

164
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,990
every side can defend tipping.

165
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,990
A worker may prefer the income tips provide.

166
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,990
A customer may want to reward attentive service.

167
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,990
A business may believe the model helps it employ

168
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,990
people while keeping the posted price attractive.

169
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,990
And service quality does matter to customers.

170
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,990
In the 2023 survey,

171
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,990
77 percent said it was a major

172
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,990
factor in deciding whether and how much to tip.

173
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,990
It would also be wrong to treat every tip screen as coercion.

174
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,990
The norm changes with the setting.

175
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,990
Among Americans who used each service in that survey,

176
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,990
92 percent of sit-down restaurant diners said they

177
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,990
always or often tipped.

178
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:00,990
The figure was

179
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,990
25 percent when buying coffee

180
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,990
and 12 percent at fast-casual restaurants.

181
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,990
There is no single tipping rule.

182
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,990
That uncertainty is part of the mechanism.

183
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:11,990
Now stress test the system by

184
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,990
imagining the tip button disappears tomorrow.

185
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,990
The work still has to be paid for.

186
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,990
If tips had been supporting worker income,

187
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,990
that compensation would need another source.

188
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:24,990
A business might use higher posted prices,

189
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,990
different wages, a service charge,

190
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,990
or some combination.

191
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,990
The evidence here does not tell us exactly

192
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,990
how much prices or wages would change.

193
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,990
It tells us that removing the visible tip

194
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,990
does not remove the underlying labor cost.

195
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,990
It would change who has to name that cost.

196
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:43,990
With a posted price or mandatory charge,

197
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,990
the business makes the pricing decision before you pay.

198
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:47,990
With a tip,

199
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,990
part of the decision arrives after the service,

200
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,990
and you are asked to complete it.

201
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,990
The business sets the stage.

202
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,990
The platform frames the options.

203
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,990
The worker lives with the result.

204
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,990
You deliver the final number.

205
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,990
That is the full human price of tipping.

206
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:04,990
You pay money,

207
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,990
but you also pay attention.

208
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,990
You stop, calculate,

209
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:09,990
read the room,

210
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:11,990
interpret an uncertain norm,

211
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,990
and decide what fairness should cost

212
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:15,990
in this particular encounter.

213
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:16,990
The worker receives income,

214
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,990
but may also receive uncertainty

215
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,990
tied to customer traffic, context,

216
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:22,990
and custom.

217
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,990
The employer gains flexibility,

218
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,990
but leaves part of compensation exposed to

219
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,990
thousands of individual judgments.

220
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,990
Then the screen turns back around.

221
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,990
The coffee is still the same coffee.

222
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,990
The button is still optional.

223
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,990
But now you can see what the transaction is asking you to do.

224
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,990
You are not merely rewarding service.

225
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:42,990
For a few seconds,

226
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:44,990
you are being asked to price fairness

227
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,065
inside a system you did not design.
